 1	\section{The closed carrier and its active core}
 2	For $n\ge1$ put $V=[n]$ and $X_n=V^2\times V^V$. On the full labelled
 3	carrier, with ordered registers, define
 4	\begin{equation}\label{eq:update}
 5	 R(u,v,f)=(v,f(v),f[v:=u]).
 6	\end{equation}
 7	Every expression on the right uses the old state. We allow $u=v$, loops and
 8	arbitrary total maps $f$; there is no nil value, stopping rule or hidden edge
 9	identity. We determine two kinds of information: every exact period, and
10	the orbit decorations needed for the labelled census. They share a return-map
11	analysis, but a scalar period does not determine the decorations.
12	
13	The kernel is established in-place list reversal, as in Manna and
14	Waldinger~\cite{manna1987}. Loginov, Reps and Sagiv~\cite{loginov2007}
15	analyze a NULL-initialized, NULL-stopped version on possibly cyclic lists,
16	including restored handles. Berdine et al.\ explicitly use the decreasing
17	quantity $2i+j+k$ in their panhandle example~\cite[Example~8]{berdine2006}.
18	Thus neither reversal nor doubled-handle timing is claimed as new.
19	
20	Rotor routing also has a complete established orbit theory. On a fixed finite
21	strongly connected directed multigraph, with prescribed cyclic orders, let
22	$T_G(v)$ count inward spanning arborescences and $M=\gcd_v T_G(v)$.
23	Pham's theorem gives $M$ recurrent rotor orbits, each of size
24	$\sum_v d^+(v)T_G(v)/M$~\cite[Theorem~1]{pham2015}; the size is independent
25	of the chosen cyclic orders. The Eulerian tour case is also treated in
26	Holroyd et al.\ \cite[Theorem~3.8]{holroyd2008}.
27	These are not our contribution. A barbell with cycle lengths $3,3$ and bridge
28	length $1$ has pointer period $16$ below, whereas its bidirected Eulerian
29	rotor graph has $14$ arcs and recurrent period $14$. This excludes only a
30	time-preserving identification on that same core, not factors, suspensions,
31	enlarged states or arbitrary rotor encodings.
32	
33	Some elementary facts fix the setting. The inverse is
34	\begin{equation}\label{eq:inverse}
35	 R^{-1}(a,b,g)=(g(a),a,g[a:=b]).
36	\end{equation}
37	Substitution proves both identities, including coincident registers. Hence
38	every state is periodic. A fixed state has $u=v=f(v)$; the other pointers
39	are arbitrary, giving $n^n$ fixed states. These facts receive no separate
40	contribution claim.
41	
42	Associate to $(u,v,f)$ the undirected multigraph $G$ with the stored edges
43	$\{x,f(x)\}$ for all $x$, and one extra edge $\{u,v\}$. Edges have
44	multiplicity but no identities; a loop contributes two to degree. Represent
45	the extra edge by $u\to v$ and the other edges by their stored arrows.
46	Equation~\eqref{eq:update} exchanges the stored and extra roles of two
47	edges and reverses the traversed extra arrow, so $G$ is invariant.
48	
49	\begin{lemma}\label{lem:core}
50	The component containing the registers has a connected two-core $K$ with
51	$s$ vertices, $s+1$ edges and minimum degree two. Both registers stay in
52	$K$. Every pointer outside $K$ is frozen. The core is a figure-eight with
53	cycle lengths $a,b$, a barbell with cycle lengths $a,b$ and bridge length
54	$c$, or a theta with path lengths $a,b,c$, all lengths positive.
55	Their sizes are respectively $a+b-1,a+b+c-1,a+b+c-1$.
56	\end{lemma}
57	\begin{proof}
58	Each functional component has equally many edges and vertices. Adding the
59	register edge either augments one such component or joins two; in both
60	cases the active component has one excess edge. The active vertex $u$ has
61	two outgoing arrows, and every other vertex has one. A degree-one vertex
62	is therefore not $u$, and its single edge is its stored arrow directed
63	toward the remaining graph. Removing it removes no remaining vertex's
64	outgoing arrow. Induction through leaf pruning leaves $u$, the extra edge,
65	and hence $v$ in the two-core; all removed arrows point inward. Invariance
66	of $G$ makes those arrows, and all inactive components, frozen for all time.
67	
68	The core retains excess one, so $\sum_{x\in K}(\deg x-2)=2$.
69	There is one degree-four vertex or two degree-three vertices, all others
70	having degree two. Following chains between branch incidences gives two
71	closed chains in the first case; in the second it gives either three
72	joining chains, or one joining chain and a closed chain at each end.
73	These are exactly the asserted three shapes, with loops and length-two
74	cycles included.
75	\end{proof}

